  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Consider the following statements :</p><p>(A) The principal quantum number &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>&#39; is a positive integer with values of &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>&#39;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo></math></p><p>(B) The azimuthal quantum number &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></math>&#39; for a given &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>&#39; (principal quantum number) can have values as &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></math>&#39; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math> has&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;values.</p><p>(C) Magnetic orbital quantum number '$\\mathrm{m}$' for a particular '$\\mathrm{l}$' (azimuthal quantum number) has $(2\\mathrm{l}+1)$ values.</p><p>(D) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#177;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> are the two possible orientations of electron spin.</p><p>(E) For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math>, there will be a total of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;orbital</p><p>Which of the above statements are correct?</p>",
